<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · lake</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lake Tana</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lake Tana is the largest lake in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> and the source of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blue-nile">Blue Nile</a>. Its islands are home to ancient monasteries that are important centres of Ethiopian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. During the invasion by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-ibrahim-al-ghazi">Ahmad ibn Ibrahim</a> in the sixteenth century, monasteries around Lake Tana were among those destroyed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lake-tana.jpg" alt="Lake Tana" width="320" height="312" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lake_tana.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
